  "final_report": "[stop_reason: hop_cap_reached | hops used: 4/4]\n\nAnalyst summary:\n**Investigation Summary \u2013 Disruption at Formosa Petrochemical (first reported\u202f\u2248\u202f2019\u201104\u201107)**  \n\n**1. Structural downstream exposure (Tier\u202f1 & Tier\u202f2) identified by the supply\u2011graph traversal**\n\n| Tier | Company (sector, country) | Confidence of link to Formosa Petrochemical |\n|------|---------------------------|---------------------------------------------|\n| **Tier\u202f1** | PetroChina \u2013 Oil & Gas Producer, China | High |\n|  | Shell \u2013 Oil & Gas Producer, UK | High |\n|  | Taiwan Cement \u2013 Construction Materials, Taiwan | High |\n|  | Sumitomo Chemical \u2013 Chemicals, Japan | Medium |\n|  | JSR \u2013 Chemicals, Japan | Medium |\n|  | Formosa Chemicals\u202f&\u202fFibre \u2013 Chemicals, Taiwan | Medium |\n|  | Jiangsu Lopal Tech \u2013 Chemicals, China | High |\n|  | Zeon \u2013 Chemicals, Japan | High |\n|  \u2026 (additional Tier\u20111 names listed in the traversal) |  |  |\n| **Tier\u202f2** | Nexen Tire \u2013 Automotive, South Korea | High |\n|  | China Petroleum\u202f&\u202fChemical \u2013 Oil & Gas Producer, China | High |\n|  | Avient \u2013 Chemicals, USA | High |\n|  | POSCO \u2013 Steel, South Korea | High |\n|  | Samsung SDI \u2013 Automotive (battery cells), South Korea | High |\n|  | LG Chem \u2013 Chemicals, South Korea | Medium |\n|  | Apple \u2013 Technology Hardware, USA | High |\n|  \u2026 (many more Tier\u20112 names) |  |  |\n\nThe traversal returned **98 enriched companies** (\u2248\u202f390 reachable). The list above shows the highest\u2011confidence connections; many additional firms appear further down the ranking.\n\n**2. Evidence gathered**\n\n| Company | Source(s) consulted | What was found |\n|---------|---------------------|----------------|\n| **PetroChina** | `get_supplier_info` (20\u2011F filing, 2018\u201104\u201127) \u2013\u202fpre\u2011event filing | The filing contains the usual corporate description, segment breakdown (upstream, refining & chemicals, marketing etc.) but **does not mention any disruption at Formosa Petrochemical**. No explicit reference to a supply interruption in April\u202f2019 is present. |\n| **Shell** | `get_supplier_info` (20\u2011F filing, 2018\u201103\u201115) \u2013\u202fpre\u2011event filing | The annual report describes Shell\u2019s upstream/downstream operations but **contains no mention of Formosa Petrochemical or any related supply issue** around April\u202f2019. |\n| **Sumitomo Chemical** | Attempted `get_supplier_info` \u2013\u202fno filings on record | No SEC\u2011type filing exists for Sumitomo Chemical in the tool\u2019s database, so **no documentary evidence could be retrieved**. |\n| **Other Tier\u20111/Tier\u20112 firms** (e.g., Taiwan Cement, JSR, Zeon, etc.) | Not yet queried \u2013\u202ftool budget exhausted | No evidence was collected; their possible exposure remains **unconfirmed**. |\n\n**3. What could not be confirmed**\n\n* **Direct impact on any downstream customer** \u2013 The two filings examined (PetroChina and Shell) are dated before the reported disruption and contain no forward\u2011looking statements about supply interruptions at Formosa Petrochemical. Consequently, we cannot confirm that these companies actually experienced material effects (e.g., reduced feedstock, production cuts, contract breaches).  \n* **Sumitomo Chemical** \u2013 No filing was available; therefore we have no documentary proof of any effect on or from Sumitomo Chemical.  \n* **All other listed Tier\u202f1 and Tier\u202f2 firms** \u2013 Because the investigation reached its four\u2011turn limit before additional `get_supplier_info` or targeted `search_corpus` calls could be made, there is **no evidence either supporting or refuting** their exposure. Their inclusion in the graph indicates a plausible supply relationship, but we lack concrete confirmation.  \n\n**4. Overall assessment**\n\nThe supply\u2011graph shows that a disruption at Formosa Petrochemical potentially touches a wide range of downstream users across chemicals, oil & gas, construction materials, automotive batteries and even consumer electronics (e.g., Apple). However, **the limited documentary evidence obtained so far does not verify any actual operational or financial impact on those companies**. The two major oil\u2011major filings reviewed predate the event and are silent on the matter; Sumitomo Chemical has no filing in the database, and all other candidates remain unverified.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \n- **Potentially affected firms (high confidence):** PetroChina, Shell, Taiwan Cement, Jiangsu Lopal Tech, Zeon, POSCO, Samsung\u202fSDI, Apple, etc.  \n- **Evidence status:** Only pre\u2011event corporate filings for PetroChina and Shell were retrieved; they contain no mention of the Formosa disruption. No evidence was found for Sumitomo Chemical or any other downstream firm due to tool limits.  \n\nFurther research (e.g., targeted news searches, later SEC filings, press releases after April\u202f2019) would be required to determine whether the identified companies actually suffered supply shortages, production delays, or financial losses because of the Formosa Petrochemical disruption.\n\nCOVERAGE GAPS (96) -- reported explicitly, not omitted:\n  - sumitomo chemical: no evidence gathered\n  - jsr: no evidence gathered\n  - formosa chemicals & fibre: no evidence gathered\n  - taiwan cement: no evidence gathered\n  - jiangsu lopal tech: no evidence gathered\n  - zeon: no evidence gathered\n  - dl holdings co lirl: no evidence gathered\n  - nan ya plastics: no evidence gathered\n  - formosa plastics: no evidence gathered\n  - taita chemical: no evidence gathered\n  - bp: no evidence gathered\n  - glencore: no evidence gathered\n  - nexen tire: no evidence gathered\n  - china petroleum & chemical: no evidence gathered\n  - perusahaan perseroan persero pt perusahaan listrik negara: no evidence gathered\n  - 